### [We're doomed.]($section.id('2025-04-12T00:00:00').data('author','xq'))

GUI branch news flash: We have a "game", running with 3 FPS on the RP2350:

> []($block.attrs('pictures','large'))
> []($image.siteAsset('img/devlog/2025/2025-04-12_dungeon.webp').linked(true))

> []($block.attrs('note'))
> The "game" is actually a port of the raycaster from my demo [Burn It Down!](https://demozoo.org/productions/230575/)
> running on an LPC1768 with 100 MHz and 2 × 32 kB of RAM.
>
> The low frame rate on the RP2350 is due to the demo rendering in column-major order,
> bottom-left to top-right. The PSRAM cache really doesn't like this access order and
> each pixel written is basically a cache miss.

### [Case Assembly]($section.id('2025-04-09T00:00:00').data('author','xq'))

The metal parts I've ordered in [January](/devlog/2025-q1#2025-01-11T00:00:00) finally arrived
and I had time to assemble it:

> []($block.attrs('pictures'))
> []($image.siteAsset('img/devlog/2025/2025-04-09_case-1.webp').linked(true))
> []($image.siteAsset('img/devlog/2025/2025-04-09_case-2.webp').linked(true))
> []($image.siteAsset('img/devlog/2025/2025-04-09_case-3.webp').linked(true))

I also figured the PCBs need guides so you can hit the slot in the case much easier.

A friend 3D printed them, but they are a bit too tight in fit.

A problem now is that as soon as the front plate is in, it's basically
locked tightly and you cannot remove it without a suction plate or shoving
the PCB out through an open slot next to it.


